{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 8 'allocator|lifecycle|verify-issued|verify-unissued|abandon|gap|terminal|exhaust' collab/adversarial-dao-jing-v2-delta-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"3-Completed-Local: 2026-09-07 00:59:43 +07 (Asia/Bangkok)\n4-Coding-Agent: glm\n5-Session-ID: 50d48a71-9ff9-44b7-8dc0-b334e5f42aac\n6-\n7-# Delta review — dao.jing.v2 plan, revision 3 → revision 4\n8-\n9-Verified cell-by-cell against `rpc.cljc` (r1 read, re-confirmed on the two load-bearing mechanics this round). Cites are revision 4.\n10-\n11:## 1. Is the table exhaustive and correct?\n12-\n13:**My P1's fix is correctly applied.** Registration at any id-bearing `request!` outcome (`requested`/`pending-request`/`request-undeliverable`, each of which does carry `:dao.stream.rpc/id` — verified), the record's removal as the exactly-once guard, the sweep assigned to order 4, and order 5's drain-after-registration so an undeliverable hop's already-outboxed completion routes in the same step. All three loss windows I filed — undeliverable at issue, undeliverable on the order-1 re-attempt of a deferred hop, driver `abandon` during a deferred hop — now have rows (13, 18) and tests. The three added tests assert exactly what I demanded: one materialize `:lost`, no phantom get completion, no residual record.\n14-\n15-**Every *impossible* cell is truly unreachable, not merely unconsidered:**\n16-\n17-- `:put` + get-id completion (row 9): no get id exists while `:put` — true, the get is only allocated at order 4 after the put completes.\n18:- `:verify-unissued` + any RPC completion (row 16): the put id already completed when the record left `:put`, and each id completes once; no get id exists yet — true.\n19:- `:verify-issued` + order-4 issuance or sweep (row 23): true by order 4's own guard, and the exit exists (rows 17–18, lose-outstanding).\n20:- `:verify-issued` + put-id completion (row 24): true — the put id completed `:present` to leave `:put`, and `handle-event` removes an id from `:outstanding` when it responds, so a second put-id completion is structurally impossible.\n21:- *removed* + any completion for a formerly registered id (row 25): true — a request id reaches a completion through exactly one path (undeliverable branch consumes `:unsent`; response consumes the `:outstanding` entry; `lose-outstanding`/`abandon-unsent` consume their own slot), so double completion is impossible, and the defensive behavior is specified.\n22-\n23:**Routing ids match what `rpc.cljc` puts on each completion.** Rows 2, 5–8 route by put id — the completions there are built from the put's request record; rows 13, 18–22 route by get id — built from the get's request record; the published materialization always carries the put id, consistently with the header's rule. The footnote's exhaustiveness claim (\"every completion carries either a response or a reason,\" `rpc.cljc:128-134`; every `request!` outcome covered with `invalid-request` unreachable because the materializer always supplies keyword op + vector args) is accurate.\n24-\n25:**But there is one event the table has no row for — and it is the fourth instance of the recurring defect class this table was built to end.** Filed as the P2 below: `allocator-error` is the one terminal in `rpc.cljc` that does not run `lose-outstanding` first (`allocation-failure` at rpc.cljc:156–161 sets `:terminal` and appends only a diagnostic — just re-verified), and once terminal, `poll!` returns without reading, so outstanding ids are never completed, and `rebind` refuses because the terminal is not `/detached`. The table handles allocator-error only for the record being allocated (rows 3 and 14). Every *other* record whose hop is outstanding — a `:put` record awaiting its put response, a `:verify-issued` record awaiting its get — is stranded: no completion will ever arrive for its id, order 3 has no unsent to abandon, the order-4 sweep touches only `:verify-unissued`, and `abandon` is a no-op on an outstanding (not unsent) envelope. No row, no exit. The plan's own allocator-error test cannot detect this, because it scripts the error without a bystander record.\n26-\n27:**No wrong reachable cell found.** I walked the four sub-events of row 5 (order-1 undeliverable, driver abandon, order-3 abandon, lose-outstanding including the non-terminal `:gap` variant), the mid-loop edge where allocator-error sets terminal during order 4's issuance loop (remaining unissued hops are swept by the *next* step's terminal branch — deferred, not lost), and the two-hops-pending case where the first hop's `full` stops the loop (row 12; nothing pending a drain is missed because `pending-request` appends no completion). All consistent.\n28-\n29-## 2. Does the phase split hold?\n30-\n31:**Yes, and the boundary is exactly right.** `:verify-issued` begins at any id-bearing `request!` outcome — including `pending-request`, while the envelope is still the RPC layer's `:unsent` — because that is precisely the moment the record becomes *routable*: from then on any completion carrying that id can be matched, and every way that id can complete is a table row (requested/undeliverable via order 1, `/abandoned` via order 3 or driver `abandon`, response via order 2). An earlier boundary (`:verify-issued` only on `requested`) would reopen my r3 P1's window; a later one doesn't exist. The \"unissued only\" sweep leaves no issued record exitless: issued-and-`unsent` exits through order 3's `abandon-unsent` (row 18), issued-and-outstanding through `lose-outstanding` at the terminal event (row 18) — with the sole exception of the allocator-error terminal, which is the P2.\n32-\n33-## 3. Is the order-4-then-order-5 claim sound?\n34-\n35:**Yes.** `attempt-unsent`'s undeliverable branch appends the completion to `:completed` synchronously inside the `rpc/request!` call order 4 makes; order 5 then takes and routes it — same step, no deferred visibility. The `full` case the prompt flags is the easy one: `pending-request` appends *nothing* (rpc.cljc:207–210 returns state unchanged apart from the result), so a loop stopped by `full` leaves nothing in the outbox that order 5 needed to see. The case needing care is allocator-error mid-loop, which sets terminal during order 4: the in-flight record is synthesized `:lost` (row 14), the remaining unissued hops are swept on the next step's terminal branch, and the outstanding bystanders are the P2.\n36-\n37-## Findings\n38-\n39:**P2 | Decision 3 lifecycle table + step order 4 (r4 hunk @382) | `allocator-error`'s terminal orphans every record whose hop is outstanding — the table has no row for it, and no order completes it.** `allocation-failure` (rpc.cljc:156–161) sets `:terminal :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error` *without* `lose-outstanding` — it is the only terminal path that strands `:outstanding` (every other terminal runs `lose-outstanding` first; just re-verified). Once terminal, `poll!` returns without reading (rpc.cljc:429–430) and `rebind` refuses (allocator-error is not `/detached`), so the stranded ids never complete. Bystander records in `:put` (awaiting the put response) or `:verify-issued` (awaiting the get) therefore have no completion, no exit, and no row: order 3 has no unsent, the order-4 sweep is unissued-only, and `abandon` is a no-op on an outstanding envelope. The table claims exhaustiveness and its allocator-error test scripts the error without a bystander, so the hole is silent under the plan's own test. Mitigation, stated honestly: the trigger is practically unreachable in single-driver usage — id exhaustion needs ~9e15 allocations, and collision requires `:next-id` to name an in-use id, which monotonic allocation plus per-step drained completions prevents — but the plan treats allocator-error as a first-class cell (rows 3, 14, and its test), so consistency requires the bystander cell be handled or explicitly disclaimed. **Fix:** generalize the terminal sweep — on a terminal whose arrival did *not* lose outstanding (allocator-error specifically, detectable as the order-4/request outcome or the terminal value), complete every remaining record `:lost` with that reason by put id; or add the row as a named, disclaimed rpc-layer defect (\"allocator-error strands outstanding requests at the RPC layer; recorded for `dao.stream`, not cured here\") — either closes it; and extend the allocator-error test with one outstanding bystander put asserting it completes `:lost` exactly once (or asserting the disclaimed behavior).\n40-\n41:**P3 | Decision 3 lifecycle table preamble (r4 hunk @382) | The preamble's \"every event that can reach it\" overclaims: three benign no-op events have no rows.** Driver `abandon` during `:verify-unissued` (no envelope of this record exists; the abandon completes the *unrelated* unsent); driver `abandon` during `:verify-issued`-with-get-outstanding (no-op; row 18 covers only the unsent case); a response-medium `gap` arriving while `:verify-unissued` (non-terminal, completes only outstanding ids, none of this record's). Each is genuinely a no-op for the record — the omissions are harmless — but a reader verifying exhaustiveness against the table, as the preamble instructs, cannot distinguish \"no row because impossible\" from \"no row because unconsidered,\" which is precisely the ambiguity this round's allocator-error hole exhibits. **Fix:** a one-line footnote listing the no-op events per phase, or explicit no-op rows. Related nit in the same table: the \"routes by\" column is defined as \"the id the RPC completion carries,\" but rows 14 and 15 are *synthesized* completions with no RPC completion to carry an id — say \"synthesized; published under the put id\" in those cells.\n42-\n43-## Does this close?\n44-\n45:**Yes — this closes, with one small disposition owed.** My r3 P1 is fixed correctly and completely (registration-at-any-id, removal-as-guard, assigned sweep, same-step drain, three tests that assert the exact invariants I demanded), the P3 is fixed verbatim, and the two additions beyond my fix — the phase split and the lifecycle table — are the right structures, correctly executed: every impossible cell I could attack held, every reachable cell's routing id matches the source, and the phase boundary is exactly the routability boundary. The residuals are one completeness hole in the table's own claim (P2 — practically unreachable, one line to fix or disclaim, and the only one I would want answered before implementation) and one editorial P3. Nothing in these 374 lines makes the plan worse than revision 3; the verify-hop lifecycle is now specified to a standard the three prior rounds' prose never reached.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
