{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 8 'allocator|lifecycle|verify-issued|verify-unissued|abandon|gap|terminal|exhaust' docs/design/dao.jing.v2.implementation-plan.md\"","aggregated_output":"36-`observe-step!`) has **no consumer under `src/`**. Every caller is a test. The\n37-`dao.stream` require at `jing.cljc:19` exists solely for `ds/next` at\n38-`jing.cljc:391`, which only tests reach.\n39-\n40-## The problem, by piece\n41-\n42-| piece                                                    | v1 mechanism                                                                                               | v2 counterpart                                                            |\n43-| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n44:| `dao.jing/observer-state`, `observe-step!`               | `ds/next` on any v1 reader; inline `{:position 0}` cursors; bare `:ok`/`:blocked`/`:end`/`:daostream/gap`  | reader surface: `cursor`, `next`; opaque cursors; outcome maps            |\n45-| `dao.jing.file/create-content-file`                      | `ds/open!` of `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; `ds/next` to replay; `ds/append!` + fsync; `ds/close!`     | **none** — the stream plan defers file/log transports                     |\n46-| `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`, `content-client`     | `dao.stream.rpc.client/call!` — synchronous on the JVM, Promise/Future elsewhere; JVM-only constructor     | `dao.stream.rpc` — poll-shaped, state-machine-valued, all three hosts  |\n47-| `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers`                       | handler map `{op (fn [& args] value)}`                                                                     | same shape; `dao.stream.apply/dispatch-request` applies it unchanged   |\n48-| `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.coordinate`, hashing, `materialize!`, `get`, `close!` | stream-free                                                                         | unchanged                                                                 |\n49-\n50-Consumers outside `dao.jing*` that reach these pieces, named so the boundary is\n51-visible. Nine `src/` namespaces require `dao.jing` — `dao.data.btree.storage`,\n52-`dao.space.index`, `dao.space.schema`, `dao.jing.{file,mem,remote,dht,dht.node}`\n--\n92-\n93-No compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol. The v1 observer and v1\n94-remote are evidence about behavior, not constraints on the v2 shape.\n95-\n96-## Decisions\n97-\n98-Settled here so no phase decides them alone.\n99-\n100:### Decision 1 — The observer: entry shape, cursor discipline, gap\n101-\n102-**Entry shape.** A pool member is `{:stream <v2 reader handle>, :cursor <opaque>,\n103-:status s}`. The composition mints the cursor and hands both in:\n104-\n105-```clojure\n106-(observer/observer-state [{:stream h1 :cursor c1} {:stream h2 :cursor c2}])\n107-;; => {:members [{:stream h1 :cursor c1 :status :pending} ...] :next 0}\n108-```\n--\n119-\n120-**Cursor discipline.** The observer retains exactly the successor `next`\n121-returns, after `materialize!` succeeds, and never anything else. There is no\n122-arithmetic on a cursor anywhere in `dao.jing.v2.observer`, and no test inspects\n123-a cursor's shape — the conformance harness's own rule. The only other way a\n124-member's cursor changes is `adopt-cursor`:\n125-\n126-```clojure\n127:(observer/adopt-cursor state i cursor) ; cursor came from the stream: minted, successor, or gap recovery\n128-```\n129-\n130-which is the composition passing back a value the stream handed out.\n131-\n132-**Signals are the contract's outcome set, total.** `observe-step!` returns\n133-`{:state s' :signal k ...}` where `k` is one of the seven keywords in\n134-`dao.stream/outcomes-next`, and no other:\n135-\n136-| signal                                                   | when                                                                   | extra keys                          | member cursor |\n137-| -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------- |\n138-| `:dao.stream/ok`                                         | a payload was materialized                                             | `:address`                          | successor     |\n139-| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                    | the pool is empty, or every non-ended member is blocked                | —                                   | unchanged     |\n140-| `:dao.stream/end`                                        | every member has ended                                                 | —                                   | unchanged     |\n141:| `:dao.stream/gap`                                        | member `i`'s position was evicted                                      | `:member i`, `:cursor` (recovery)   | unchanged     |\n142-| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed                                   | `:member i`, `:result` (the outcome map) | unchanged |\n143-\n144-The last four are reported immediately, the member's `:status` records the\n145-outcome, the scheduling index moves past that member, and the member is polled\n146:again on its next turn — and reports again. That is v1's gap rule (\"never\n147-auto-resynchronized\") applied to every non-`ok`/`blocked`/`end` outcome, one\n148:rule instead of four. A `gap` on a content intake means payloads were appended,\n149-acknowledged to their publisher, and never materialized. Materialization is\n150-idempotent and replay-safe, so adopting the recovery cursor is *safe*; whether\n151-it is *acceptable* only the composition can answer, since it knows whether the\n152-publisher will re-append. The observer must not answer it. `cursor-mismatch`\n153-and `invalid-cursor` are composition defects; `transport-error` may be\n154-transient. All three are resolved the same way: the composition adopts a cursor\n155-it obtained from the stream, or rebuilds the pool without that member. A test\n156-iterates `dao.stream/outcomes-next` and asserts every outcome produces a\n--\n170-sentence is generalized to the three defect outcomes; *Implemented surface* —\n171-names `dao.jing.observer` and the stream-free file backend (see Decision 2 for\n172-the design-document change that entails); *Open items* — durable checkpoints\n173-gain the sentence that a checkpoint stores whatever cursor value the transport\n174-minted, and that whether such a value survives serialization is transport-owned\n175-and TBD in the contract. Stays, untouched: *Definition*, *Publication from an\n176-agent*, *Materialization rule*, *Canonical encoding*, *Storage ignorance*,\n177-*Physical intake versus semantic composition*, *Reads* (one clause added, see\n178:J4), *Resource lifecycle*, *Lineage*. Nothing semantic about DaoJing moves; the\n179-migration changes how the observer reads, not what it is.\n180-\n181-### Decision 2 — `dao.jing.file`: the durable log was never a stream (option b)\n182-\n183-**Decision.** The file backend's durable log is a storage implementation\n184-detail. The migration removes the `dao.stream` coupling; it does not build a\n185-v2 append-log transport, and it does not use one.\n186-\n--\n195-order of weight:\n196-\n197-1. **The log has no second reader and no second writer, ever.** Inside\n198-   `create-content-file` the stream is opened, replayed once by its own\n199-   handle, appended by its own `put`, and closed by its own `close`. No\n200-   cursor is handed out, no descriptor is projected, nothing attaches, and\n201-   the log never sits in any pool. Every property the contract exists to\n202-   guarantee — independent cursors, non-destructive reads, portable\n203:   reachability, attachment lifecycle — is unused.\n204-2. **Making the on-disk layout a `dao.stream` transport made DaoJing's storage\n205-   format a public transport type.** `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` is a\n206-   name any composition may open, so the record framing of a content file\n207-   became something other code could depend on. Removing the transport makes\n208-   the framing private to `dao.jing.file`.\n209-3. **A transport with one consumer owes the conformance suite for nobody.**\n210-   Option (a) would have this plan own a v2 append-log, its manifest, its\n211-   exclusion reasons, its inducers, and its reader laws on three hosts — for a\n--\n311-\n312-**Client side, J3a — backend primitives, stepped by the composition.**\n313-\n314-```clojure\n315-(remote/client-state rpc-state)                ; wraps dao.stream.rpc client state; no cursor is fabricated\n316-(remote/request-put state address payload)     ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n317-(remote/request-get state address)             ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n318-(remote/step state budget)                     ; => {:state s' :attempt k? :completions [...] :diagnostics [...]}\n319:(remote/abandon state reason)                  ; => s'\n320-```\n321-\n322-- `request-put` and `request-get` validate the address as `jing/get` does —\n323-  a non-segment address is a defect in the caller and throws before any\n324-  request is allocated — then call `rpc/request!`. Their `:outcome` is the\n325-  RPC layer's outcome set **verbatim**: `requested`, `pending-request` (the\n326-  writer answered `full`; the envelope is retained), `request-undeliverable`,\n327:  `allocator-error` (mints no id; its diagnostic is retained), and `terminal`.\n328-  One addition: while `rpc/unsent?` holds, both return\n329-  `:dao.jing.v2.remote/busy` and allocate nothing, because `rpc/request!`\n330-  would silently retry the unsent envelope and ignore the new operation.\n331-- **`step` is the only path that clears `busy`.** Its order is fixed:\n332:  1. if `:unsent` is held and the state is not terminal, re-attempt it through\n333-     `rpc/request!` (whose unsent branch ignores op/args, `rpc.cljc:243-244`)\n334-     and fold that outcome — `requested`, `pending-request`, or\n335-     `request-undeliverable`, the last surfacing as a `:lost` completion —\n336-     into the step result under `:attempt`;\n337-  2. `rpc/poll!` with the budget;\n338:  3. if the returned state is terminal and still holds `:unsent`,\n339:     `rpc/abandon-unsent` with the terminal reason, so the never-accepted\n340-     request completes as `:lost` through the ordinary outbox rather than\n341-     being carried silently across a later `rebind`;\n342:  4. **the materializer's turn** (J3c below): if the state is not terminal,\n343-     issue unissued verify hops in put-id order until the writer answers\n344:     `full` or none remain; if the state is terminal, complete every\n345:     unissued verify hop as `:lost` with the terminal reason;\n346-  5. take completions and diagnostics exactly once, routing each completion\n347-     whose id a materialization record knows into that record (order 4's\n348-     issuance registers ids before this drain, so an undeliverable hop's\n349-     completion, already in the outbox, routes).\n350-\n351-  A driver that only re-calls `request-*` can never clear a `busy`; it must\n352-  step. Nothing here loops on `blocked`, holds a clock, retries on its own, or\n353-  touches a socket. Timeouts, reattachment (`rpc.ws/rebind` on `/detached`),\n354-  cadence, and what to do with a lost request are the driver's.\n355:- **`abandon state reason`** is the composition's tool for its own\n356-  disconnect-before-rebind — an operator disconnect, a rebind onto a fresh\n357-  attachment — mirroring `yin.repl.driver` (`driver.cljc:220,258,293,305`),\n358:  which abandons before every rebind. It calls `rpc/abandon-unsent`; the\n359:  completion carries `:dao.stream.rpc/abandoned` when no terminal reason\n360:  exists. Ids stay monotonic. The abandoned envelope's completion routes into\n361:  a materialization record if its id is registered there (see the lifecycle\n362-  table), on the next `step`'s drain.\n363-- **Completion decode is total by construction.** A completion carrying\n364-  `:dao.stream.rpc/response` decodes by op: `{:id n :op :jing/put-content\n365-  :result :inserted}` or `:present`; `{:id n :op :jing/get-content :found?\n366-  true :value v}` or `:found? false`; a response that is not the exact\n367-  presence envelope, or not one of `:inserted`/`:present`, or an error\n368-  response, becomes `{:id n :op … :error {code message}}` — the malformed\n369-  cases under `:dao.jing.v2.remote/malformed-response`, as data, because it\n370-  surfaces inside a driver step. A completion carrying\n371-  `:dao.stream.rpc/reason` becomes `{:id n :op … :lost reason}` for **any**\n372:  reason keyword, pass-through, qualified keyword unchanged: `:dao.stream/gap`,\n373-  `:dao.stream/end`, `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch`, `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`,\n374:  `:dao.stream/transport-error`, the lifecycle `:dao.stream.apply/detached`,\n375-  `/ended`, `/not-found`, `/transport-error`, an undeliverable append's\n376:  outcome, and `:dao.stream.rpc/abandoned` are examples, not a closed\n377:  vocabulary. Reader `:dao.stream/end` and lifecycle `/ended` stay distinct\n378-  because the keyword is never rewritten.\n379-- **`:diagnostics`** is part of the step result and is published exactly once:\n380:  decode errors, unsolicited responses, events after terminal, transport\n381-  diagnostics. A caller that discards them discards them knowingly.\n382-- The namespace is pure `.cljc` with no host branch. The v1 network client was\n383-  JVM-only; the v2 client runs on all three hosts by construction.\n384-\n385-**Client side, J3c — the stepped materializer.**\n386-\n387-```clojure\n388-(remote/request-materialize state payload)     ; => {:outcome k :state s' :id n?}\n389-```\n390-\n391-Derives the address locally with `jing/segment-key`, issues `:jing/put-content`\n392-through `request-put`, and — **as soon as `rpc/request!` returns an id**,\n393-whatever the outcome — records `{:payload p :address a :phase :put}` under\n394-that put id. `requested`, `pending-request` and `request-undeliverable` all\n395:carry `:dao.stream.rpc/id` (`rpc.cljc:198,203,211`); only `allocator-error`\n396:and `terminal` mint no id, and then no record is created and the outcome is\n397-returned as-is. On an `:inserted` completion, completes `{:id n :op\n398-:materialize :address a :result :inserted}`. On `:present`, the materialization\n399-needs a second correlated `:jing/get-content` for the same address, and the\n400:record moves to `:verify-unissued`. When that get completes, the\n401-materialization completes `:present` with the address only if the returned\n402-value equals the payload; otherwise an `:error` completion carrying\n403-`:dao.jing.v2.remote/integrity-failure` (unequal) or `/present-but-absent`\n404-(`:found? false`) — the two failures `jing.cljc:283-293` throws on locally, as\n405-data here. A `:lost` on either hop completes the materialization as `:lost`\n406-with the reason. **One completion per materialization**: hop completions are\n407-consumed by the record and never republished as put or get completions. The\n408-driver holds the put id and correlates by it. Read-side hydration needs only\n409-J3a; `store-tree-async` depends on J3c.\n410-\n411-**Who issues the verify hop: `step` does, and this is the one obligation the\n412-client carries.** The `:present` completion is decoded inside `step`, and the\n413-get it calls for cannot always be issued at that moment — the writer may\n414-answer `full`, or an unsent envelope may already hold the slot. So `step`\n415:retains the unissued hop as data in the per-id record (`:verify-unissued`) and\n416-issues it in step order 4 on this or a later step, when `rpc/unsent?` is\n417-false: it calls `rpc/request!` for `:jing/get-content` and, **as soon as an id\n418-is returned — on `requested`, `pending-request` or `request-undeliverable`\n419-alike** — registers the same record under that get id, moving the record to\n420:`:verify-issued`. From then on the record is reachable by **both** the put id\n421-and the get id, and every completion carrying either id routes to the\n422-materialization rather than to a caller's own `request-get`, including a\n423-completion that was already sitting in the outbox when the id was returned\n424-(an undeliverable hop) and one that arrives later (a `full`-deferred hop\n425:abandoned or lost). If the writer answers `full`, the get becomes the RPC\n426:layer's `:unsent`, which step order 1 re-attempts next time. On a terminal\n427:state, order 4 completes every `:verify-unissued` record as `:lost` with the\n428:terminal reason; `:verify-issued` records need no sweep, because their get is\n429-either outstanding — completed by `lose-outstanding` — or `:unsent` —\n430:completed by order 3's `abandon-unsent` — and either completion routes by the\n431-registered get id. **The record's phase transition to done is the\n432-exactly-once guard**: the first completion that reaches a record completes the\n433-materialization and removes the record from both indices; a later completion\n434-for a removed id is impossible from the RPC layer, which completes each id\n435-once, and would otherwise be published as an ordinary unrouted completion,\n436-never a second materialization completion.\n437-\n438:**The lifecycle, as a table.** Every state the record can be in, every event\n439-that can reach it, what happens to the record, what is published, and under\n440:which id it routes. A reader checks exhaustiveness against this table, not\n441-against prose. \"Routes by\" names the id the RPC completion carries; the\n442-published materialization completion always carries the **put id**.\n443-\n444-| record phase | event | record | published | routes by |\n445-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n446-| *(none)* | `request-materialize`: `rpc/request!` returns `requested` or `pending-request` | created, `:phase :put`, registered under put id | nothing (outcome returned to caller) | — |\n447-| *(none)* | `request-materialize`: `rpc/request!` returns `request-undeliverable` | created, `:phase :put`, registered under put id; the completion is already in the outbox | on the next drain: `{:id put :op :materialize :lost reason}`; record removed | put id |\n448:| *(none)* | `request-materialize`: `allocator-error` or `terminal` | not created | nothing; outcome returned to caller, no id | — |\n449-| `:put` | order 1 re-attempts the put (it was `:unsent`): `requested` / `pending-request` | unchanged | nothing | — |\n450:| `:put` | order 1 re-attempt undeliverable, or driver `abandon` while the put is `:unsent`, or order 3 abandons it on terminal, or `lose-outstanding` loses it | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :lost reason}` | put id |\n451-| `:put` | put completion `:inserted` | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :address a :result :inserted}` | put id |\n452:| `:put` | put completion `:present` | `:phase :verify-unissued` | nothing | put id |\n453-| `:put` | put completion `:error` (handler error, malformed response) | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :error e}` | put id |\n454-| `:put` | a get-id completion | **impossible** — no get id has been allocated | — | — |\n455:| `:verify-unissued` | order 4, not terminal, `rpc/unsent?` true (another envelope holds the slot) | unchanged; retried next step | nothing | — |\n456:| `:verify-unissued` | order 4, not terminal, `rpc/request!` for the get returns `requested` | `:phase :verify-issued`, registered under get id | nothing | — |\n457:| `:verify-unissued` | order 4, `rpc/request!` returns `pending-request` (writer `full`) | `:phase :verify-issued`, registered under get id; the envelope is the RPC `:unsent`; order 4 stops issuing further hops this step | nothing | — |\n458:| `:verify-unissued` | order 4, `rpc/request!` returns `request-undeliverable` | `:phase :verify-issued`, registered under get id; the completion is already in the outbox | on order 5's drain, same step: `{:id put :op :materialize :lost reason}`; record removed | get id |\n459:| `:verify-unissued` | order 4, `rpc/request!` returns `allocator-error` | removed (the RPC state is now terminal; no id exists to route) | `{:id put :op :materialize :lost :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error}` | put id |\n460:| `:verify-unissued` | order 4, state terminal (the sweep) | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :lost terminal-reason}` | put id |\n461:| `:verify-unissued` | any RPC completion | **impossible** — the put id already completed (`:present`) and no get id exists | — | — |\n462:| `:verify-issued` | order 1 re-attempts the get (it was `:unsent`): `requested` / `pending-request` | unchanged | nothing | — |\n463:| `:verify-issued` | order 1 re-attempt undeliverable, or driver `abandon` while the get is `:unsent`, or order 3 abandons it on terminal, or `lose-outstanding` loses it | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :lost reason}` | get id |\n464:| `:verify-issued` | get completion `:found? true`, value `=` payload | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :address a :result :present}` | get id |\n465:| `:verify-issued` | get completion `:found? true`, value `≠` payload | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :error /integrity-failure}` | get id |\n466:| `:verify-issued` | get completion `:found? false` | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :error /present-but-absent}` | get id |\n467:| `:verify-issued` | get completion `:error` (handler error, malformed response) | removed | `{:id put :op :materialize :error e}` | get id |\n468:| `:verify-issued` | order 4 issuance or terminal sweep | **impossible** — order 4 acts only on `:verify-unissued`; an issued hop completes through its registered get id | — | — |\n469:| `:verify-issued` | a put-id completion | **impossible** — the put id completed when the record left `:put` | — | — |\n470-| *removed* | any completion for a formerly registered id | **impossible** from the RPC layer, which completes each id once; defensively, published as an ordinary unrouted completion, never a second materialization completion | — | — |\n471-\n472-Every RPC completion carries either a response or a reason (`rpc.cljc:128-134`),\n473:so the \"completion\" rows above are exhaustive over what `poll!`, `request!`,\n474:`abandon-unsent` and `lose-outstanding` can emit; every `rpc/request!` outcome\n475:(`requested`, `pending-request`, `request-undeliverable`, `allocator-error`,\n476:`terminal`, `invalid-request`) is covered, `invalid-request` being unreachable\n477-because the materializer always supplies a keyword op and a vector of args.\n478-\n479-This spends a principle knowingly. Round 1 refused to queue caller work inside\n480-the client (contested item 6): a queue of *submitted operations* awaiting a\n481-slot would hide the driver's ordering decisions and grow without bound. The\n482-verify hop is neither. It is the second half of one operation the caller\n483-already submitted and holds an id for; it is bounded by the number of\n484-materializations in flight, each of which already consumed an id; it admits no\n485-new caller work; and it is data in the state the driver owns, visible as\n486:`:verify-unissued` or `:verify-issued`, not a hidden buffer. It is also the\n487-shape the RPC layer already sanctions twice: `dao.stream.rpc`'s own\n488-`:unsent` and `apply/serve-once!`'s `:pending-response` are machine-internal\n489-obligations retained as data and driven by the caller's next call\n490-(`rpc.cljc:259-264`, `apply.cljc:301-315`), so a client carrying one is\n491-pattern-consistent, not exception-carved. The alternative — a public\n492-`resume-materialize` the driver must call — would make the driver responsible\n493-for re-driving an intent it never expressed and cannot see, and re-calling\n494-`request-materialize` mints a fresh put and orphans the original record. The\n--\n553-\n554-## Divergence register\n555-\n556-Every place the v2 pieces deliberately differ from v1, with the reason.\n557-\n558-| v1 behavior                                                                                      | v2                                                                                                   | why                                                                                                   |\n559-| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n560-| `observer-state` takes streams; cursor is `{:position 0}`                                        | takes `{:stream :cursor}` members; the composition mints the cursor                                  | every valid cursor comes from the stream; the anchor is the composition's policy                       |\n561:| signals `:ok`, `:blocked`, `:end`, `:daostream/gap`; malformed results throw                     | the seven `:dao.stream/…` outcomes; three defect outcomes reported as data with `:member` and `:result` | the result convention; an outcome outside the closed set still throws, as a transport defect         |\n562:| a gap is reported, cursor unchanged, never resynced                                              | same rule, extended to `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`; `adopt-cursor` is the resync | one rule for every non-progress outcome; resync is a composition decision                         |\n563-| observer lives in `dao.jing`                                                                     | v1 observer moves to `dao.jing.observer`; v2 observer is `dao.jing.v2.observer`                      | `dao.jing` must be stream-free so nine production requirers do not carry a stream dependency          |\n564-| `dao.jing.file` opens `{:dao.stream/type :append-log}`; handle carries `:log`                    | private framed file; handle carries `:path :state :write-lock` and the three fns, no `:log`          | Decision 2. **Recorded dissent:** `gpt-5.6-sol` holds that direct file IO behind `:put-content-fn` violates `datom.world.md:53-68` (a host boundary is a stream boundary; callable adapters are rejected) and that a v2 append-log is constructible under the no-wait rule and should be built. The plan holds that the violation, if it is one, is the synchronous content handle itself, present today and unchanged by any transport beneath it, and that a constructible append-log cannot supply `:inserted`'s durability meaning without redesigning `materialize!` and its consumers. `glm-5.3` and `gpt-6-astra` accept the plan's position. |\n565-| `file_test` counts records with `ds/next` over the raw log                                       | `dao.jing.file/records` returns the decoded records of a path; tests use it                          | the framing is private; the test reads through the backend's own reader                              |\n566-| `content-client` is a handle; `:get-content-fn` returns the value                                | `client-state` / `request-*` / `step`; results are completions correlated by id                       | no operation waits; unimplementable otherwise on cljs/cljd                                            |\n567:| `jing/materialize!` over a remote handle                                                         | `request-materialize`: derive, put, verify on `:present`, complete with the address; `step` issues the verify hop and owns its lifecycle | the integrity behavior of `jing.cljc:276-293` must survive the loss of the handle |\n568-| collision or unreadable-present throws                                                           | `:error` completion with `/integrity-failure` or `/present-but-absent`                              | inside a driver step an exception has nowhere to go                                                   |\n569-| `connect-content!` — synchronous, JVM-only                                                       | gone; the composition attaches through `dao.stream.ws` and builds the client from the whole `attach!` result | the constructor waited; attachment outcome arrives as deposited data                         |\n570-| malformed presence envelope throws inside `get`                                                  | completes as an `:error` with a malformed-response code                                              | same                                                                                                  |\n571-| `close-fn` guards concurrent closes with a lock and retries on failure                           | gone with the handle; `close!` on the ws handle is idempotent and answers `ok`                       | the client holds no host resource; the handle does                                                    |\n572-| `:dao.jing/remote` coordinate opens to a handle                                                  | unchanged on v1; no v2 coordinate in this plan                                                       | it would open into a composition, which is `dao.space`'s to design with hydration                     |\n573-\n574-**v1-only tests deliberately not mirrored:** `file_test`'s direct\n575-`{:dao.stream/type :append-log}` opens and `count-records` (replaced by\n--\n584-\n585-## Phase J0 — What exists\n586-\n587-Recorded so the phases start from evidence.\n588-\n589-- `dao.stream` (protocols, outcome sets, `validate-outcome`), the ring\n590-  buffer with `make-attacher`, `dao.stream.apply` (envelope, `serve-once!`),\n591-  `dao.stream.rpc` (`client-state`, `request!`, `poll!`, `take-completed`,\n592:  `take-diagnostics`, `unsent?`, `abandon-unsent`, `rebind`),\n593-  `dao.stream.rpc.ws` (`decoder`, `init-client`, `rebind`),\n594-  `dao.stream.ws` with host `connect!` seams on clj, cljs and cljd,\n595-  `dao.stream.serving`, and the conformance harness. All green per the user\n596-  on this revision.\n597-- `dao.jing` core is stream-free except for the observer; `dao.jing.mem` is\n598-  stream-free. The v1 observer has no `src/` caller.\n599-- `test/dao/jing_test.cljc` observer cases (lines 322–560) are the behavioral\n600-  evidence for J2; `test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc` and\n--\n736-`stream/cursor … :dao.stream/oldest`:\n737-\n738-- every observer case from the former `jing_test.cljc` lines 322–560, ported:\n739-  plain-data state, empty pool blocked, all blocked, hashing and retrieval,\n740-  equal payloads from two streams converge, blocked/ended members do not\n741-  starve others, all ended, drains then end, fair round-robin, strict\n742-  interleave, cursor advances only after successful materialization. Cursor\n743-  assertions become successor-equality assertions, never position arithmetic.\n744:- **gap**: a capacity-2 ring buffer with three appends; the signal is\n745:  `:dao.stream/gap` with `:member 0` and a `:cursor`; the member cursor is\n746:  unchanged; the other member still progresses; the gap reports again on the\n747-  next turn; after `adopt-cursor` with the recovery cursor the member\n748-  materializes the live value.\n749-- **defect outcomes**: a reified reader handle scripted to answer\n750-  `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error` (and, for the\n751-  totality test, each outcome in `dao.stream/outcomes-next`); assert the\n752-  signal, `:member`, `:result`, unchanged cursor, and that an outcome outside\n753-  the declared set throws.\n754-- **members round-trip**: `(observer-state (remove-nth (:members st) i))`\n--\n761-`dao.jing.v2*` requires `dao.stream` **directly or transitively** — or, under\n762-the documentation route, `dao.jing.v2.observer` requires `dao.stream`\n763-transitively through `dao.jing` until J5, and this deliverable says so.\n764-\n765-## Phase J3 — `dao.jing.v2.remote`\n766-\n767-**J3a — backend primitives, socket-free, all three hosts.**\n768-`src/cljc/dao/jing/v2/remote.cljc`: `default-handlers`, `client-state`,\n769:`request-put`, `request-get`, `step`, `abandon`, per *Decision 3*. Tests,\n770-`test/dao/jing/v2/remote_test.cljc`, mirror `rpc_test.cljc`: request and\n771-response media are two ring buffers; the server side is `apply/server-state`\n772-+ `apply/serve-once!` over `default-handlers` on a `dao.jing.mem` store; the\n773-client is `rpc/client-state` over the same media, wrapped by `client-state`.\n774-\n775-- put then get round-trips through `step`; duplicate put completes `:present`;\n776-  absent get completes `:found? false`; a stored `nil` and a stored\n777-  `{:found? true :value \"hello\"}` round-trip exactly.\n--\n781-  once: `pending-request`; a second request returns `busy` and allocates\n782-  nothing; `step` re-attempts and its result carries `:attempt requested`;\n783-  exactly one id allocated; then a case where the driver only re-calls\n784-  `request-*` and asserts `busy` persists.\n785-- **unsent at detach**: a request left `pending-request`, then `/detached`\n786-  deposited on the response medium; `step` produces exactly one `:lost`\n787-  completion for it with `/detached` as the reason, and `:unsent` is empty in\n788-  the returned state; after `rebind` the old envelope is never sent.\n789:- **`abandon`** before an explicit rebind completes the unsent request with\n790:  `:dao.stream.rpc/abandoned`; ids remain monotonic.\n791:- gap on the response medium completes every outstanding request as `:lost`\n792:  with `:dao.stream/gap`; reader `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor` and\n793-  `end` complete as `:lost` with those exact keywords.\n794:- `allocator-error` on an exhausted or colliding allocator returns that\n795-  outcome, mints no id, and its diagnostic appears in the next `step`'s\n796-  `:diagnostics`.\n797-- malformed responses complete as `:error` with the malformed-response code\n798-  and never as `:found?`; unsolicited responses and decode errors appear in\n799-  `:diagnostics`.\n800-- **declaration-driven totality**: every keyword `dao.stream.rpc` can emit\n801-  as an outcome or reason — enumerated from that namespace's public vars and\n802-  its documented reason set — has a branch; completions and diagnostics\n--\n816-killing the connection completes an *accepted* outstanding request as `:lost`\n817-with `/detached`, and **a kill with an unsent request** produces exactly one\n818-`:lost` completion and an empty `:unsent` after `rebind` on a fresh `attach!`,\n819-with the same deposit cursor resumed; a descriptor naming a path nothing\n820-serves completes as `:lost` with `/not-found`.\n821-\n822-**J3c — the stepped materializer.** `request-materialize` per *Decision 3*,\n823-over the J3a primitives, in the same namespace; `step` order 4 issues pending\n824:verify hops and sweeps them on terminal. Tests, socket-free, on all three\n825:hosts, one per row-group of the lifecycle table:\n826-\n827-- fresh payload: one put, completes `:inserted` with the derived address.\n828-- present payload: put answers `:present`, `step` issues the correlated get in\n829-  the same step, equality holds, completes `:present` with the address;\n830-  exactly two ids; the per-id record is reachable by both.\n831-- **verify hop deferred by `full`**: the put completes `:present` while the\n832:  writer is scripted `full`; after that step the record is `:verify-issued`\n833-  with the get as `:unsent`, no completion has been published, and a caller's\n834-  `request-get` answers `busy`; the next step's order 1 re-attempts the get\n835-  (writer `ok`); the completion arrives on the following step; exactly two\n836-  ids at completion.\n837-- **unrelated unsent request occupying the slot**: a caller's `request-get`\n838-  is left `pending-request` by a `full`; then the `:present` completion\n839:  arrives; the record stays `:verify-unissued` that step because\n840-  `rpc/unsent?` holds; the next step re-attempts the caller's unsent first\n841-  (order 1), then issues the verify hop (order 4); both complete; ids are\n842-  monotonic and the verify get's completion routes to the materialization,\n843-  not to the caller's get.\n844-- **undeliverable at issue**: the writer is scripted `closed` when order 4\n845-  issues the get; exactly one `{:op :materialize :lost :dao.stream/closed}`\n846-  completion is published in that same step's drain, carrying the put id; no\n847-  get completion is published; no record remains.\n848-- **undeliverable on the order-1 re-attempt of a deferred hop**: the get was\n849-  deferred by `full`; the writer answers `transport-error` on the next\n850-  step's order 1; exactly one materialize `:lost` completion, no phantom get\n851-  completion, no residual record.\n852:- **driver `abandon` during a deferred hop**: the get is `:unsent`; the\n853:  driver calls `abandon`; the next step's drain publishes exactly one\n854:  materialize `:lost :dao.stream.rpc/abandoned`; no phantom, no residual.\n855:- **terminal with an unissued hop**: `/detached` arrives in the same poll as\n856:  the `:present` (both in one budget), so order 4 finds the state terminal\n857:  with the record `:verify-unissued`; exactly one materialize `:lost\n858-  /detached`; no residual.\n859:- **terminal with an issued hop**: the get is outstanding when `/detached`\n860-  arrives; `lose-outstanding`'s completion routes by the get id; exactly one\n861-  materialize `:lost /detached`; the sweep does not double-complete it.\n862-- present but the server's get returns `:found? false`: completes `:error`\n863-  with `/present-but-absent`.\n864-- present but unequal (a scripted server store seeded with a different value\n865-  at the same address): completes `:error` with `/integrity-failure`.\n866:- `allocator-error` on the verify hop's allocation: exactly one materialize\n867:  `:lost :dao.stream.rpc/allocator-error`, carrying the put id.\n868-- a caller's own `request-get` for the same address during a verify hop is\n869-  not confused with it (per-id record under both ids).\n870:- **table exhaustiveness**: a test enumerates every `(phase, event)` cell the\n871:  lifecycle table marks as reachable and asserts each is exercised by one of\n872-  the cases above, so a row added to the table without a test fails.\n873-\n874-Deliverable: J3a and J3c green on clj, cljs (Node) and cljd; J3b green on clj\n875-and cljs (Node), and on cljd through the cljd ws lane. The cljd test\n876-regenerates `test/cljd-out/`; one process owns it at a time.\n877-\n878-## Phase J4 — Design prose\n879-\n--\n974-  stated in Decision 4's table, J2's deliverable, and the end condition.\n975-- `src/cljc/dao/stream/log.cljc` and `test/dao/stream/log_test.cljc`, deleted\n976-  (J1.3); alternative: stream-plan-owned \"legacy append-log retirement\",\n977-  coordinated atomically with J1.\n978-\n979-**Architectural decisions**, made here: the durable log is not a stream and\n980-the write-path redesign is a separate item (Decision 2); remote content is a\n981-driven state machine with a stepped materializer whose verify hop `step`\n982:issues and whose lifecycle is tabulated (Decision 3); the observer's cursor\n983-and non-progress discipline (Decision 1); the three-namespace end state and\n984-the observer move-out (Decision 4).\n985-\n986:**Implementation gaps**, left open knowingly: the v2 remote client has no\n987-coordinate and cannot be reached from `dao.jing.coordinate/open!`; the\n988-serving side exists only as test compositions; the promise-shaped\n989-`hydrate-async`/`store-tree-async` of `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 is a driver\n990-over this client that this plan does not build.\n991-\n992-**Intentionally deferred**, each to a named home:\n993-\n994-- **The content write path as an effect stream** — a DaoJing architecture\n--\n1027-- `dao.jing.v2.observer` observes v2 reader handles with stream-minted\n1028-  cursors, is total over `dao.stream/outcomes-next` by a\n1029-  declaration-driven test, retains only successors and adopted cursors, and\n1030-  requires only `dao.jing` and `dao.stream`.\n1031-- `dao.jing.v2.remote` round-trips put, get and **materialize** through\n1032-  `dao.stream.apply` and `dao.stream.rpc` over ring buffers on all three\n1033-  hosts and over `dao.stream.ws` on each; clears `busy` only through\n1034-  `step`; issues every deferred verify hop through `step`; completes every\n1035:  materialization exactly once through every reachable cell of the lifecycle\n1036-  table, with no phantom hop completion and no residual record; and publishes\n1037-  diagnostics exactly once.\n1038-- `dao.jing.md` describes the v2 observer, the stream-free file backend, the\n1039-  driven remote client with its materializer, and records the write-path\n1040-  question as an open item.\n1041-- No namespace under `dao.jing.v2*` requires `dao.stream` or\n1042-  `dao.stream.rpc.*`, **directly or transitively** — or, under the\n1043-  documentation route, `dao.jing.v2*` is transitively v1 until J5, stated as\n--\n1052-## What changed from revision 3\n1053-\n1054-- **The verify hop's loss path has a route home.** The record is registered\n1055-  under the get id as soon as `rpc/request!` returns one — on `requested`,\n1056-  `pending-request` and `request-undeliverable` alike, each of which carries\n1057-  `:dao.stream.rpc/id` (`rpc.cljc:198,203,211`) — and likewise under the\n1058-  put id at `request-materialize`. Every completion carrying a registered id\n1059-  completes the materialization once, the record's removal being the guard.\n1060:  The terminal sweep is assigned to step order 4 and touches only\n1061:  `:verify-unissued` records; issued hops complete through their registered\n1062:  get id via `lose-outstanding` or order 3's `abandon-unsent`. Step order 5\n1063-  is stated to drain after order 4 registers, so an undeliverable hop's\n1064-  already-outboxed completion routes in the same step.\n1065:- **A lifecycle table** enumerates every record phase (`:put`,\n1066:  `:verify-unissued`, `:verify-issued`, removed) against every event — each\n1067-  `rpc/request!` outcome at each issuance point, order-1 re-attempts,\n1068:  `abandon`, the terminal sweep, `lose-outstanding`, and each completion\n1069-  kind — with the record transition, the published completion, and the id it\n1070-  routes by; impossible cells say why. This is the structural answer to a\n1071-  defect that recurred three times as a missed exit.\n1072-- **Three loss-path tests added** (undeliverable at issue, undeliverable on\n1073:  the order-1 re-attempt of a deferred hop, `abandon` during a deferred hop),\n1074:  plus terminal-with-unissued, terminal-with-issued, `allocator-error` on the\n1075:  hop, and a table-exhaustiveness test; the deferred-by-`full` and\n1076-  slot-occupied tests restated in the table's phase vocabulary.\n1077-- **The RPC precedent is cited**: `:unsent` and `apply/serve-once!`'s\n1078-  `:pending-response` are the same shape of machine-internal obligation\n1079-  retained as data.\n1080-- **The documentation-route sentence corrected**: under that route\n1081-  `dao.jing.observer` does not exist, so none of the seven repoints happens;\n1082-  only the `jing_test` section move and the `file_test` excision do.\n1083-- Status line updated to revision 4. Nothing else changed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
